From 60e7e29a5f24219aa33b63cc5703fc965db85c8c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Fri, 11 Sep 2026 09:27:08 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/5] Keep document text and let the chat retrieve from it
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

process_document transcribes an uploaded report with Claude vision, the
extractor pulls the numbers out into health_records, and the text was then
dropped on the floor — len(text_content) was the only thing that survived. The
documents table and its ocr_text column existed in the model and were never
written to; Document( does not appear anywhere in the codebase. So everything
that is not a number — the conclusion, the prescribed medication, the reason
for a referral — was unanswerable, and the uploaded file itself is no backup:
it sits on container storage that every deploy wipes.

Uploads now store the transcription and index it:

- app/rag/retriever.py chunks a document into overlapping, paragraph-aligned
  passages and ranks them with BM25. Lexical rather than embeddings on purpose:
  the corpus is a few dozen reports, the Railway Postgres runs the stock
  postgres:17 image with no pgvector and the SQLite fallback has no vectors
  either, so this needs no extension, no new dependency and no embedding key,
  and a wrong hit can be traced to the term that matched. _score is the seam
  where embeddings slot in once the corpus stops fitting in one pass.
- Tokens are folded to ASCII and truncated to a stem, because Slovak is
  inflected and Postgres ships no Slovak dictionary: "cholesterolu" and
  "cholesterol" have to be the same key or a question never matches a report.
- document_chunks is a table rather than an on-the-fly split so the same text
  is not chunked differently between requests and a passage can be traced back
  to its document and position.
- The chat context carries both the passages retrieved for the question and a
  plain list of every stored document. Retrieval can miss; the list cannot, so
  the model stops claiming a report does not exist when it only failed to match
  the query terms. Passages sit above the daily aggregates, which are the
  bulkiest section and the one the size cap trims first.
- GET /api/upload/search runs the same retrieval, so a bad answer can be
  attributed to retrieval or to the model rather than guessed at.

The numbers stay on the SQL path. "Priemer za posledné 3 dni" is arithmetic and
must not depend on similarity search; retrieval only carries the wording around
the numbers.

Note for the existing data: the 241 records already extracted came from
documents whose text was discarded, so retrieval covers uploads from here on.
Re-uploading those reports backfills them.

